## Why This Is Asked
Designing a modular, policy-driven prompt assembly system tests understanding of multi-tenant safety, latency budgets, and deterministic prompt composition under constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Fragment catalogs with metadata: policy, latency, locale, tone
- Tenant-aware scoring and ordering under latency limits
- Guardrails and watermark injection when needed
- Audit logging with fragment IDs and decision trace

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype outline (not full implementation)
const fragments = [
  {id:'intent', text:'Explain concisely', latency:10},
  {id:'tone', text:'in a formal style', latency:5},
  {id:'locale', text:'English', latency:2}
];
function assemble(tenantPolicy, budget){
  // simplistic greedy selection
  let chosen = [];
  let used= new Set();
  for(const f of fragments){
    if(!used.has(f.id) && budget- f.latency >=0 && tenantPolicy.includes(f.id)){
      chosen.push(f); used.add(f.id);
      budget -= f.latency;
    }
    if(chosen.length>=3) break;
  }
  return chosen.map(x=>x.text).join(' ');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle non-determinism in fragment selection across model updates?
- How to validate audits for compliance and GDPR logging obligations?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests ability to design a lightweight, repeatable prompt-testing workflow and catch prompt drift early.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt versioning
- Drift metrics (embedding similarity, content guards)
- Reproducibility and logging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype skeleton for drift check
function driftFlag(basePrompt, paraphrase) {
  const baseOut = model(basePrompt)
  const paraOut = model(paraphrase)
  const sim = cosineSimilarity(embed(baseOut), embed(paraOut))
  return sim < 0.8
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this for multi-language prompts?
- How would you integrate with CI to prevent drift before deploy?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses the ability to design intelligent, cost-aware routing systems for multi-module AI architectures in real-time environments, requiring consideration of performance trade-offs, safety constraints, and practical implementation details.

## Key Concepts

- Latency budgeting and real-time performance optimization
- Cost-aware routing and resource allocation
- Safety gating and data sensitivity handling
- Modular prompt orchestration and fallback mechanisms
- Policy-based decision making with precedence rules

## Code Example

```javascript
function routePrompt(prompt, signals) {
  const { budget, latency, safety, data_sensitivity } = signals;

  // Safety-first routing
  if (data_sensitivity > 0.7 || safety > 0.5) {
    return {
      module: 'SafetyGuard',
      plan: ['analyze_content', 'apply_filters', 'return_sanitized'],
      payload: { prompt, safety_score: safety, sensitivity_level: data_sensitivity }
    };
  }

  // Cost-optimized routing
  if (budget < 0.3 || latency < 100) {
    return {
      module: 'LightSummarizer',
      plan: ['extract_key_points', 'generate_summary'],
      payload: { prompt, max_tokens: 150 }
    };
  }

  // Default to comprehensive processing
  return {
    module: 'CitationEmbedder',
    plan: ['analyze_content', 'extract_citations', 'generate_embeddings'],
    payload: { prompt, include_sources: true }
  };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a practical, risk-aware prompt orchestration layer for high-sensitivity contexts, with concrete routing logic and fail-safes.

## Key Concepts

- Prompt orchestration and modular routing
- Signal fusion (intent, sensitivity, latency)
- Safety-first edge cases and auditing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
function routePrompt(intent, sensitivity, latencyMs) {
  // simple scoring example
  const modules = {
    'SQL-builder': {score: 0, safe: latencyMs > 200},
    'Privacy-masker': {score: 2, safe: true},
    'Result-summarizer': {score: latencyMs <= 500, safe: true}
  };
  // bias on conflicts toward Privacy-masker
  const pick = latencyMs < 1000 ? 'Privacy-masker' : 'SQL-builder';
  return {module: pick, payload: {prompt: 'assembled', constraints: {maskPII: true}}};
}
```

